.TH RUNFLOW "1" "November 2026" "runflow" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
runflow \- run OPM Flow on a deck, here or as an LSF job
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runflow
[\fB\-q\fR \fIqueue\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-l\fR] [\fB\-v\fR \fIversion\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR \fIprocesses\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
\fIinput_deck\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runflow
runs a Flow version on \fIinput_deck\fR: as an LSF job (the default), or here
and now (\fB\-i\fR). It chooses the version, starts Flow with MPI when needed,
sets the version's \fBPYTHONPATH\fR, and reserves what a reservoir coupling
deck needs for its slaves.
.PP
Flow runs in the deck's directory, on \fIROOT\fB.DATA\fR, where \fIROOT\fR is
the deck's file name up to its first dot: \fBcases/A\fR, \fBcases/A.DATA\fR and
\fBcases/A.B.DATA\fR all run \fBcases/A.DATA\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Long options may also be written with one dash (\fB\-np\fR, \fB\-op\fR,
\fB\-queue\fR) and shortened while unambiguous; options may come after the deck.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-queue\fR \fIqueue\fR
The LSF queue. Default: \fBlsf.default_queue\fR in \fBrunflow.json\fR, else
LSF's default queue.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Run here, without a queue. Flow's output goes to the terminal and to the log
files.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR
List the Flow versions, with their OPM version and executable.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR \fIversion\fR
The Flow version: a path, a configured name, an executable's name (e.g.
\fBflowrc47\fR) or an OPM version (e.g. \fB2026.04\fR); see
.BR flowversions (1).
Default: \fBdefault_version\fR in \fBrunflow.json\fR, else the first
\fBflow\fR in \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-np\fR \fIprocesses\fR
Flow's MPI processes (default 1). For a reservoir coupling master, the
master's processes: the slaves' come from the deck (see below).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-op\fR \fIoptions\fR
Flow's options, as one argument split at spaces, e.g.
\fB\-o '\-\-enable\-esmry=true \-\-max\-time\-step\-in\-days=1.0'\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print what would be run: the \fBbsub\fR command line and the job script, or
the command for \fB\-i\fR. Nothing is written or run; the checks are made as
for a real run.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a short usage, with this site's defaults.
.SH RUNNING
One process runs Flow alone; more run it through an MPI launcher: the
\fBlauncher\fR program with \fBlauncher_options\fR of where it runs,
\fBlocal\fR for \fB\-i\fR (default \fBmpirun \-\-bind\-to none \-np\fR \fIN\fR),
the scheduler's section (\fBlsf\fR) in jobs (the same, and \fB\-x
OMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fR).
.PP
The launcher program is the one that belongs to the Flow version: the
version's own \fBlauncher\fR in \fBrunflow.json\fR if it has one, else the
section's. A path is used as it is; a name, e.g. \fBmpirun\fR, is found as the
shell would after \fBmodule load\fR of that Flow:
.IP 1. 4
next to the version's \fBflow\fR, e.g. \fB/usr/lib64/openmpi/bin/mpirun\fR for
a Flow from the Open MPI packages of Red Hat\-like systems;
.IP 2. 4
in \fBPATH\fR.
.PP
It is used by its absolute path, also in a job, and is checked first: its
\fB\-\-version\fR must be the MPI Flow was built with (\fBflow
\-\-build\-info\fR, \fBmpi=\fR), the same implementation and major.minor
version, or nothing runs. A Flow that does not say its MPI is not checked.
The first lines of \fIROOT\fB.LOG\fR name the launcher, how it was found and
the check.
.PP
For a job, \fBrunflow\fR writes the job script to \fIROOT\fB.job\fR and runs,
through the shell, in the deck's directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bsub \-n \fISLOTS\fR \-q \fIqueue\fR \-J \fIROOT\fR \-R 'span[hosts=1]' \-o \fIROOT\fR.%J.job.done \fIbsub_options\fR < \fIROOT\fR.job
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBbsub\fR's answer (\fBJob <\fIID\fB> is submitted to queue <\fIqueue\fB>.\fR)
appears as \fBbsub\fR prints it. The script goes on standard input, so LSF
keeps its own copy: the next job may rewrite \fIROOT\fB.job\fR.
.SH FILES
Next to the deck:
.TP
\fIROOT\fB.LOG\fR, \fIROOT\fB.ERR\fR
Flow's output and errors, written while it runs (\fBtail \-f\fR follows a
run), and overwritten by the next run, as Flow's own files are. The first
lines say where it runs, the Flow version, the processes and the command.
.TP
\fIROOT\fB.job\fR
The script of the last job submitted.
.TP
\fIROOT\fB.\fIID\fB.job.done\fR
LSF's report at the job's end.
.TP
\fIROOT\fB.runflow.log\fR
Every run appended: date, user, host, runflow's version, the Flow version
and executable, where it ran (here or LSF), the command, and its exit status
(Flow's, or \fBbsub\fR's). Dry runs and runs refused before starting are not
recorded.
.SH RESERVOIR COUPLING
A deck with \fBSLAVES\fR is a coupling master. A coupled run is several
simulations at once: the master, which \fBrunflow\fR starts, and one slave
per \fBSLAVES\fR record, which the master starts itself while it runs, each
with the processes the record gives (item 5, \fBNUM_PE\fR).
.PP
\fBrunflow\fR therefore starts the master through MPI with \fB\-n\fR
processes (default 1), and reserves room for all of them: \fB\-n\fR plus the
\fBNUM_PE\fR of every slave.
.PP
For example, a master deck with two slaves:
.PP
.RS
.nf
SLAVES
\-\- name     deck              host  directory  NUM_PE
  'RES\-1'  'RC\-01_MOD1_HIST'  '*'   '../mod1'   4 /
  'RES\-2'  'RC\-01_MOD2_HIST'  '*'   '../mod2'   1 /
/
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBrunflow RC\-01_MAST_HIST.DATA\fR runs three simulations:
.PP
.RS
.nf
master  RC\-01_MAST_HIST   1 process    (\-n 1, started by runflow)
RES\-1   RC\-01_MOD1_HIST   4 processes  (started by the master)
RES\-2   RC\-01_MOD2_HIST   1 process    (started by the master)
                          \-\-
                          6 slots
.fi
.RE
.PP
so it submits \fBbsub \-n 6\fR, and the job runs \fBmpirun \-np 1\fR for the
master alone; the slaves take the other 5 slots. With \fB\-n 2\fR, the master
has 2 processes and the job 7 slots.
.PP
If flow flags \fBSLAVES\fR as unsupported, such decks need
\%\fB\-o\ '\-\-parsing\-strictness=low'\fR.
.PP
A run that cannot hold the slaves fails before it starts: here, more slots
than this machine has cores; as a job on one host, more slots than
\fBlsf.max_slots_per_host\fR, if set.
.PP
The deck is read by the
.BR opmlaunchinfo (1)
of the chosen Flow version: the one configured for it, else one in the same
directory as its \fBflow\fR. Versions without one are read by runflow's own
parser.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fBrunflow\fR works without configuration: it finds \fBflow\fR in \fBPATH\fR,
runs \fBmpirun\fR, and submits with \fBbsub\fR and LSF's default queue. A
configuration file says what differs at a site, for a user or for a project.
\fBflowversions\fR(1) reads the same files.
.SS Files
\fBrunflow.json\fR files are read in this order; each overrides the one
before, and a missing file is skipped:
.IP 1. 4
the installation's: \fIsysconfdir\fB/opm/runflow.json\fR of the installation
\fBrunflow\fR belongs to, e.g. \fB/opt/opm/2026.04/etc/opm/runflow.json\fR, or
\fB/etc/opm/runflow.json\fR for an installation in \fB/usr\fR. The site's
settings go here;
.IP 2. 4
the user's: \fB~/.runflow.json\fR;
.IP 3. 4
the current directory's: \fBrunflow.json\fR where \fBrunflow\fR is started,
e.g. a project's settings next to its decks;
.IP 4. 4
\fB$RUNFLOW_CONFIG\fR, if set: it must exist; a relative path is taken from
the current directory.
.PP
A file only needs the settings it changes; everything else stays as the
earlier files, or the defaults, have it.
.SS Format
A file is one JSON object: settings are \fB"name": value\fR pairs, separated by
commas, in braces. Strings are in double quotes; lists are in brackets; JSON has
no comments. A setting \fBrunflow\fR does not know is reported as a warning and
otherwise ignored, so that an older \fBrunflow\fR can read a newer site's file; a
setting of the wrong type is an error that names the file.
.SS Paths and programs
.TP
\fBprograms\fR (\fBflow\fR, \fBlaunchinfo\fR, \fBlauncher\fR, \fBbsub\fR)
A value with a \fB/\fR is a path; a bare name, e.g. \fBmpirun\fR, is looked up
when it is used: a launcher next to Flow first, then in \fBPATH\fR; the others
in \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
\fBpaths and patterns\fR
A path may start with \fB~\fR, the home directory; a relative path is relative
to the directory of the file it is written in, not to where \fBrunflow\fR
runs. In patterns, \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR and \fB[...]\fR match existing files as in
the shell.
.SS Settings
Each setting with its type and default:
.TP
\fBdefault_version\fR (string; default: the first \fBflow\fR in \fBPATH\fR)
The Flow version used without \fB\-v\fR: anything \fB\-v\fR accepts, e.g. a
name from \fBversions\fR, an executable's name (\fBflowrc47\fR) or an OPM
version (\fB2026.04\fR).
.TP
\fBscheduler\fR (string; default: \fB"lsf"\fR)
The system jobs (runs without \fB\-i\fR) go to, whose section holds its
settings. Only \fB"lsf"\fR for now.
.TP
\fBsearch\fR (object)
Where versions that \fBversions\fR does not name are found. Its fields mirror
those of a version, with patterns instead of values:
.RS
.TP
\fBflow\fR (list of patterns; default: none)
Every executable these patterns match is a Flow version, selectable by its
file name, e.g. \fB["/project/res/bin/flow*"]\fR for a directory of
\fBflowrc46\fR, \fBflowrc47\fR, ... Patterns match executables, not
directories: for all of a directory's, end the pattern with \fB/flow*\fR.
\fBflow\fR in \fBPATH\fR is always looked at too.
.TP
\fBpythonpath\fR (list of patterns; default: none)
The \fBPYTHONPATH\fR directories of every version without its own
\fBpythonpath\fR. In each pattern, \fB{name}\fR is the file name the version was
found as (e.g. \fBflowrc47\fR). A pattern that matches one directory adds it;
one that matches none is left out; one that matches several is left out with a
warning. For example,
\fB["/project/res/lib/python3.*/site\-packages/{name}"]\fR finds each
version's bindings under the Python it was built for.
.RE
.TP
\fBversions\fR (object; default: none)
Named Flow versions: each key is the name given to \fB\-v\fR, each value an
object with these fields:
.RS
.TP
\fBflow\fR (program; required)
The executable.
.TP
\fBlaunchinfo\fR (program)
Its \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR(1), if it is not in the same directory as \fBflow\fR
(as in build trees); without one, runflow reads coupled decks with its own
parser.
.TP
\fBlauncher\fR (program)
Its MPI launcher, if it is not the one the \fBlocal\fR or scheduler section
finds (see RUNNING), e.g. because the one in \fBPATH\fR belongs to another MPI.
The section's \fBlauncher_options\fR stay.
.TP
\fBpythonpath\fR (list of paths)
Directories put before \fBPYTHONPATH\fR when this version runs, e.g. for
Python in decks (PYACTION). It wins over \fBsearch.pythonpath\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBlocal\fR (object)
Runs here (\fB\-i\fR):
.RS
.TP
\fBlauncher\fR (program; default: \fB"mpirun"\fR)
The MPI launcher.
.TP
\fBlauncher_options\fR (list of strings; default: \fB["\-\-bind\-to", "none", "\-np", "{np}"]\fR)
Its options, one word each; see Launcher options.
.RE
.TP
\fBlsf\fR (object)
LSF jobs:
.RS
.TP
\fBbsub\fR (program; default: \fB"bsub"\fR)
The \fBbsub\fR to run, e.g. \fB"/global/bin/bsub"\fR.
.TP
\fBbsub_options\fR (list of strings; default: none)
Options added to every \fBbsub\fR, one word each, e.g.
\fB["\-P", "reservoir"]\fR.
.TP
\fBdefault_queue\fR (string; default: LSF's default queue)
The queue of jobs without \fB\-q\fR.
.TP
\fBsingle_host\fR (true or false; default: \fBtrue\fR)
Whether all of a job's slots must be on one host (\fBbsub \-R
\(aqspan[hosts=1]\(aq\fR).
.TP
\fBmax_slots_per_host\fR (positive integer; default: no limit)
The slots one host has. A job with \fBsingle_host\fR that needs more (e.g. a
coupled run) is refused before it is submitted.
.TP
\fBlauncher\fR (program; default: \fB"mpirun"\fR)
The MPI launcher inside jobs.
.TP
\fBlauncher_options\fR (list of strings; default: \fB["\-\-bind\-to", "none", "\-np", "{np}", "\-x", "OMP_NUM_THREADS=1"]\fR)
Its options; see Launcher options.
.RE
.SS Launcher options
\fBlauncher_options\fR are the launcher's options, one word each.
\fB{np}\fR, which must be in them, becomes the number of processes to start;
\fB{slots}\fR, if used, the slots the job reserved. For example, to start
Open MPI 5 on one host with its traffic on the loopback interface, which
coupled runs may need, keeping the site's options:
.PP
.RS
.nf
"local": { "launcher_options": ["@inherit", "\-\-mca", "btl_tcp_if_include", "lo"] }
.fi
.RE
.SS How files combine
A setting a file does not give stays as the earlier files had it. A setting
it gives is combined with the earlier one by its type:
.TP
\fBobjects\fR
merge by key: the file, \fBsearch\fR, \fBversions\fR, \fBlocal\fR, \fBlsf\fR. A
user adds a version, or a project changes the queue, without repeating the
site's other settings.
.TP
\fBvalues\fR
replace the earlier value, e.g. \fBdefault_version\fR, \fBlsf.launcher\fR.
.TP
\fBlists\fR
replace the earlier list, e.g. \fBsearch.flow\fR, \fBlsf.bsub_options\fR. An
entry \fB"@inherit"\fR stands for the earlier list, at the place it is put, as
\fB$PATH\fR does in \fBPATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH"\fR: with the site's
\fB["/project/res/bin/flow*"]\fR, a user's \fB["~/opm/builds/*/flow",
"@inherit"]\fR searches the user's builds, then the site's. In the first file
that gives the setting, \fB"@inherit"\fR stands for nothing; a second one in a
list is ignored, with a warning.
.PP
One exception: a version in \fBversions\fR replaces the earlier version of the
same name whole, not field by field, since its fields describe one executable
together.
.SS Examples
A user's \fB~/.runflow.json\fR that adds an own build, selected with
\fB\-v dev\fR, and keeps the site's versions:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "versions": {
    "dev": { "flow": "~/opm/build/opm\-simulators/bin/Release/flow",
             "launchinfo": "~/opm/build/opm\-common/bin/Release/opmlaunchinfo" }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A site file for an LSF cluster that does what the legacy runflow script did:
a directory of Flow versions, each with its Python bindings under the Python
it was built for, Open MPI's \fBmpirun\fR, and \fBbsub\fR in
\fB/global/bin\fR:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "default_version": "flowrc45",
  "search": {
    "flow":       ["/project/res/x86_64_RH_8/bin/flow*"],
    "pythonpath": ["/project/res/x86_64_RH_8/lib/python3.*/site\-packages/{name}"]
  },
  "local": { "launcher": "/usr/lib64/openmpi/bin/mpirun" },
  "lsf": {
    "bsub": "/global/bin/bsub",
    "default_queue": "daily",
    "max_slots_per_host": 128,
    "launcher": "/usr/lib64/openmpi/bin/mpirun"
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A site with OPM's Open MPI packages of a Red Hat\-like system; the
\fBmpirun\fR next to Flow is found by itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "search": { "flow": ["/usr/lib64/openmpi/bin/flow*"] },
  "default_version": "2026.04"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A project's \fBrunflow.json\fR next to its decks, with its own queue and an
account for every job:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "lsf": { "default_queue": "long", "bsub_options": ["@inherit", "\-P", "field\-x"] }
}
.fi
.RE
.SS Checking a configuration
\fBflowversions\fR lists the versions the configuration gives, and
\fBflowversions show\fR \fIversion\fR what is known about one, including its
launcher and \fBopmlaunchinfo\fR. \fBrunflow \-\-dry\-run\fR \fIdeck\fR shows
what would run or be submitted, without doing it. Both print the warnings of
the files they read, e.g. unknown settings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
With \fB\-i\fR, Flow's exit status. For a job, \fBbsub\fR's (0 when
submitted; 127 if \fBbsub\fR is not found). Otherwise 1 if the Flow version is
not found, or for any other error (with its reason); 2 for wrong options; 4
without exactly one deck, or if the deck does not exist.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR flowversions (1),
.BR opmlaunchinfo (1),
.BR bsub (1)
